## Why This Is Asked
Tests a non-trivial partition DP with a non-linear subarray cost and reconstruction.

## Key Concepts
- DP over partitions
- Range maximum queries (precomputation or on-the-fly)
- Path reconstruction via predecessor table

## Code Example
```javascript
function minCostPartition(A, k) {
  const n = A.length;
  // precompute max for all ranges
  const mx = Array.from({length: n}, ()=> Array(n).fill(0));
  for (let i=0;i<n;i++){ mx[i][i]=A[i]; for(let j=i+1;j<n;j++) mx[i][j]=Math.max(mx[i][j-1], A[j]); }
  const dp = Array.from({length: n+1}, ()=> Array(k+1).fill(Infinity));
  const prv = Array.from({length: n+1}, ()=> Array(k+1).fill(-1));
  dp[0][0]=0;
  for(let i=1;i<=n;i++) dp[i][1] = mx[0][i-1]*(i);
  for(let j=2;j<=k;j++){
    for(let i=j;i<=n;i++){
      for(let t=j-1;t<=i-1;t++){
        const cost = dp[t][j-1] + mx[t][i-1]*(i-t);
        if(cost < dp[i][j]){ dp[i][j]=cost; prv[i][j]=t; }
      }
    }
  }
  // reconstruction omitted for brevity
  return {cost: dp[n][k], cutPoints: []};
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- Can you optimize space to O(n) per k?
- How to handle equal costs and tie-breaking?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests DP over partitioning with a non-trivial segment value (max letter value).

## Key Concepts
- DP over partitions with exact k segments
- Precomputing segment values (maxChar)
- Reconstructing the cut points

## Code Example
```javascript
function maxPartitionValue(s, k){
  const n = s.length;
  const val = [...s].map(c => c.charCodeAt(0) - 96); // a=1..z=26
  const maxChar = Array.from({length: n+1}, () => Array(n+1).fill(0));
  for(let i=0;i<n;i++){
    let m=0;
    for(let j=i;j<n;j++){
      m = Math.max(m, val[j]);
      maxChar[i][j+1] = m;
    }
  }
  const NEG = -1e15;
  const dp = Array.from({length: n+1}, () => Array(k+1).fill(NEG));
  const pre = Array.from({length: n+1}, () => Array(k+1).fill(-1));
  dp[0][0] = 0;
  for(let i=1;i<=n;i++){
    for(let t=1;t<=Math.min(i,k);t++){
      for(let p=t-1;p<=i-1;p++){
        const cand = dp[p][t-1] + maxChar[p][i];
        if(cand > dp[i][t]){ dp[i][t] = cand; pre[i][t] = p; }
      }
    }
  }
  // reconstruction omitted for brevity
  return {value: dp[n][k], cuts: []};
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you handle k=1 or k=n in constant time?
- Can you reduce space to O(nk) without losing reconstruction capabilities?

## Why This Is Asked
This tests minimax-style DP with reconstruction for a classic game.

## Key Concepts
- Two-player DP with difference state
- Subarray DP and reconstruction path
- Time: O(n^2) space: O(n^2)

## Code Example
```javascript
// dp[i][j] denotes max diff for i..j
function maxFirstPlayer(values){
  const n = values.length; const dp = Array.from({length:n},()=>Array(n).fill(0));
  let total = values.reduce((a,b)=>a+b,0);
  for(let i=0;i<n;i++) dp[i][i]=values[i];
  for(let len=2; len<=n; len++){
    for(let i=0;i+len-1<n;i++){
      const j=i+len-1;
      dp[i][j] = Math.max(values[i]-dp[i+1][j], values[j]-dp[i][j-1]);
    }
  }
  return (total + dp[0][n-1])/2;
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you reconstruct the exact L/R sequence from the DP table?
- How does this change if moves include taking from both ends with a coin flip to decide who starts?

## Why This Is Asked
This tests modeling of selecting multiple disjoint subarrays with a fixed cost and a cap on count, including interval reconstruction. It blends standard DP on subarrays with a knapsack-like limit and requires careful state design.

## Key Concepts
- DP over prefix index i and subarray count t
- Prefix sums for fast subarray sums
- Transitions: skip vs end-subarray
- Reconstruction via back-pointers; space/time trade-offs

## Code Example
```javascript
function maxKSubarrays(A, k, c) {
  const n = A.length;
  const pref = new Array(n+1).fill(0);
  for (let i=1;i<=n;i++) pref[i] = pref[i-1] + A[i-1];
  const dp = Array.from({length: n+1}, ()=> Array(k+1).fill(-Infinity));
  const par = Array.from({length: n+1}, ()=> Array(k+1).fill(null));
  dp[0][0] = 0;
  for (let i=1;i<=n;i++){
    for (let t=0;t<=k;t++){
      // skip i
      dp[i][t] = dp[i-1][t];
      par[i][t] = {type:'skip'};
      if (t>0){
        for (let L=1; L<=i; L++){
          const prev = dp[i-L][t-1];
          if (prev <= -Infinity/2) continue;
          const cand = prev + (pref[i]-pref[i-L]) - c;
          if (cand > dp[i][t]){
            dp[i][t] = cand;
            par[i][t] = {type:'end', end:i, len:L};
          }
        }
      }
    }
  }
  // reconstruction would walk back from (n, argmax_t)
  return {maxValue: dp[n].reduce((a,b)=> Math.max(a,b), -Infinity)};
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you optimize to O(nk) time and O(nk) space, and then reduce to O(nk) time and O(k) space?
- How would you extend for at most k subarrays with variable per-subarray costs or negative values?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests ability to design an optimized DP on a circular constraint and track the number of optimal solutions.

## Key Concepts
- DP on linear sequences with spacing (dp[i] = max(dp[i-1], dp[i-d] + v[i]))
- Reducing circularity to two linear cases (include first vs exclude first)
- Counting distinct optimal subsets with tie handling (sum counts when equal)

## Code Example
```javascript
function linearDP(arr, d){
  const n = arr.length; const dp = Array(n+1).fill(0); const ways = Array(n+1).fill(0);
  dp[0] = 0; ways[0] = 1;
  for(let i=1;i<=n;i++){
    const take = arr[i-1] + (i-d>=0 ? dp[i-d] : 0);
    const skip = dp[i-1];
    if(take>skip){ dp[i]=take; ways[i]=(i-d>=0 ? ways[i-d] : 1); }
    else if(take<skip){ dp[i]=skip; ways[i]=ways[i-1]; }
    else { dp[i]=skip; ways[i] = ((i-d>=0 ? ways[i-d] : 1) + ways[i-1]); }
  }
  return [dp[n], ways[n]];
}

function maxCircular(v, d){
  const [bestA, cntA] = linearDP(v.slice(1), d); // exclude first
  const n = v.length;
  const seg = (n > 2*d-1) ? v.slice(d, n-d+1) : [];
  const [bestBseg, cntBseg] = seg.length ? linearDP(seg, d) : [0,1];
  const bestB = v[0] + bestBseg;
  if(bestA > bestB) return [bestA, cntA];
  if(bestB > bestA) return [bestB, cntBseg];
  return [bestA, cntA + cntBseg];
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you extend to allow a variable distance per pair or weighted distance penalties?
- How would you test correctness and performance on worst-case inputs?

## Why This Is Asked

Tests a fresh DP angle: combine subproblem best within intervals with partition into exactly k blocks, ensuring non-overlap and order.

## Key Concepts

- Precompute bestSubarray(l, r) for all intervals.
- Knapsack-style DP over blocks: dp[i][t] = max over l of dp[l-1][t-1] + bestSubarray(l, i).
- Reconstruction: store the chosen l for each dp[i][t].
- Complexity: O(n^2 k) time, O(n^2) for bestSubarray and O(nk) for DP.

## Code Example

```javascript
function solve(A, k) {
  const n = A.length;
  // 1) bestSubarray(l, r)
  const best = Array.from({ length: n }, () => Array(n).fill(-Infinity));
  for (let l = 0; l < n; l++) {
    let maxEnding = 0;
    let bestSoFar = -Infinity;
    for (let r = l; r < n; r++) {
      maxEnding = Math.max(A[r], maxEnding + A[r]);
      bestSoFar = Math.max(bestSoFar, maxEnding);
      best[l][r] = bestSoFar;
    }
  }
  // 2) DP over i, t
  const dp = Array.from({ length: n }, () => Array(k + 1).fill(-Infinity));
  const par = Array.from({ length: n }, () => Array(k + 1).fill(-1));
  for (let i = 0; i < n; i++) dp[i][1] = best[0][i], par[i][1] = -2;
  for (let t = 2; t <= k; t++) {
    for (let i = 0; i < n; i++) {
      let bestVal = -Infinity, bestL = -1;
      for (let l = t - 1; l <= i; l++) {
        const cand = (l - 1 >= 0 ? dp[l - 1][t - 1] : 0) + best[l][i];
        if (cand > bestVal) { bestVal = cand; bestL = l; }
      }
      dp[i][t] = bestVal;
      par[i][t] = bestL;
    }
  }
  // reconstruction omitted for brevity
  return { bestValue: dp[n - 1][k], blocks: [] };
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you optimize memory to O(nk)?
- How would you adapt if blocks must be exactly length L or gaps have a minimum size?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests DP formulation for selecting multiple non-overlapping subarrays, a common beginner challenge with practical reconstruction.

## Key Concepts
- dp[i][t]: best sum using first i elements with exactly t subarrays
- end[i][t]: best subarray sum ending at i-1 for t subarrays
- Transitions combine extending a subarray or starting a new one
- Reconstruction via backtracking boundaries

## Code Example
```javascript
function maxKSubarrays(A, k) {
  const n = A.length;
  const NEG = -Infinity;
  const dp = Array.from({length: n+1}, () => Array(k+1).fill(NEG));
  const end = Array.from({length: n+1}, () => Array(k+1).fill(NEG));
  for (let i = 0; i <= n; i++) dp[i][0] = 0;
  for (let i = 1; i <= n; i++) {
    for (let t = 1; t <= k; t++) {
      end[i][t] = Math.max(end[i-1][t] + A[i-1], dp[i-1][t-1] + A[i-1]);
      dp[i][t] = Math.max(dp[i-1][t], end[i][t]);
    }
  }
  return {best: dp[n][k]};
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you adapt for at-most-k subarrays?
- How would you retrieve the actual subarray boundaries efficiently?

## Why This Is Asked
New angle adds a cooldown constraint to the classic k-transaction DP, testing multi-state reasoning and recovery of the trade sequence.

## Key Concepts
- DP with cooldown using rest/hold/sold states
- Tracking transaction counts and path reconstruction
- Space optimization to O(k)

## Code Example
```javascript
function maxProfitWithCooldown(prices, k) {
  const n = prices.length;
  const INF = -1e15;
  const rest = Array.from({ length: n }, () => Array(k + 1).fill(INF));
  const hold = Array.from({ length: n }, () => Array(k + 1).fill(INF));
  const sold = Array.from({ length: n }, () => Array(k + 1).fill(INF));
  rest[0][0] = 0;
  hold[0][0] = -prices[0];
  for (let i = 1; i < n; i++) {
    for (let t = 0; t <= k; t++) {
      rest[i][t] = Math.max(rest[i - 1][t], sold[i - 1][t]);
      hold[i][t] = Math.max(hold[i - 1][t], rest[i - 1][t] - prices[i]);
      if (t > 0) sold[i][t] = hold[i - 1][t - 1] + prices[i];
    }
  }
  // reconstruction omitted for brevity
  return Math.max(...rest[n - 1]);
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you extract the exact trade days from the DP tables?
- How would the solution change if cooldown length varied to c days?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests classic DP with an additional dimension (at most k intervals) and reconstruction—a common pattern in scheduling optimization problems.

## Key Concepts
- Weighted interval scheduling with a cardinality constraint (at most k intervals)
- Predecessor p(i) computed via binary search on sorted end times
- DP table with an extra dimension tracking the number of selected intervals
- Backtracking reconstruction to output the chosen jobs

## Code Example
```javascript
function maxValueKJobs(jobs, k) {
  const sorted = jobs.map((j, idx) => ({ ...j, idx })).sort((a, b) => a.end - b.end);
  const n = sorted.length;
  const ends = sorted.map(j => j.end);

  const p = (i) => {
    let lo = 0, hi = i - 1;
    while (lo <= hi) {
      const mid = Math.floor((lo + hi) / 2);
      if (ends[mid] <= sorted[i].start) lo = mid + 1;
      else hi = mid - 1;
    }
    return hi;
  };

  const dp = Array.from({ length: n + 1 }, () => Array(k + 1).fill(0));
  for (let i = 1; i <= n; i++) {
    for (let t = 1; t <= k; t++) {
      const prev = p(i - 1) + 1;
      dp[i][t] = Math.max(dp[i - 1][t], dp[prev][t - 1] + sorted[i - 1].value);
    }
  }

  const selected = [];
  let t = k;
  for (let i = n; i > 0 && t > 0; i--) {
    if (dp[i][t] !== dp[i - 1][t]) {
      selected.push(sorted[i - 1]);
      t--;
    }
  }

  return { value: dp[n][k], jobs: selected.reverse() };
}
```

